    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque.jpg" alt="Afonso de Albuquerque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Afonso_de_Albuquerque_(ap%C3%B3s_1545)_-_Autor_desconhecido.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Johor Sultanate was the direct heir of the Malacca Sultanate, the great Malay trading state of the fifteenth century. When the Portuguese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-bahmani">Mahmud Shah</a> fled south, setting up his court first in Pahang and then on the island of Bintan, from which he harassed the Portuguese until they destroyed his base in 1526. He died at Kampar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> around 1528. One of his sons, Muzaffar Shah, went on to found the sultanate of Perak; another, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-riayat-shah-ii">Alauddin Riayat Shah II</a>, established a new court on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-river">Johor River</a> at the southern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. The new state claimed Malacca's royal line, its regalia and court traditions, and its authority over the Malay lands around the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> River basin, the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau</a> archipelago, parts of the east coast of Sumatra and, at times, Pahang. Its early capitals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-lama">Johor Lama</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-sawar">Batu Sawar</a>, lay on the Johor River, but the court moved frequently in response to attack, and from the late seventeenth century its centre of gravity shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a>. The sultanate thus began as a continuation of Malacca in exile, competing to win back the trade that the Portuguese had seized.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bugis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bugis.jpg" alt="Bugis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IMG-20190222-WA0014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rejalobo, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> before it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> lived by trade. Its rulers sought to attract merchants to their river and island ports by offering security, moderate duties and access to the produce of the region: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, forest products such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a> and rattan, and the marine goods gathered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a>, the sea peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau</a> islands, whose fleets were the backbone of the sultan's power at sea. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> in 1641, Johor's ports flourished as centres of the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the archipelago, and in the late seventeenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> was among the busiest Malay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôts</a>. Malay society was organised around the ruler and the great officials, with commoners bound to chiefs and many slaves and debt-bondsmen, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> provided the common faith of court and people. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> leadership in the eighteenth century, Riau grew rich on gambier and pepper planted by Chinese settlers and on the transit trade. In the nineteenth century the temenggongs of Johor opened the mainland to Chinese pepper and gambier planters under the <em>kangchu</em> system, in which river concessions were granted to Chinese headmen; later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-palm">oil palm</a> plantations and, after independence, industry linked to neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> transformed the economy of modern Johor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raja-kecil.jpg" alt="Raja Kecil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jirat_Makam_Marhum_Buantan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harris Est 13, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>'s military strength lay at sea. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a> provided crews and fleets of oared vessels, and the sultan's warfare consisted largely of naval raids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockades</a> and the defence of fortified river capitals. For its first century Johor was caught in a three-way struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> and the Aceh Sultanate. The Portuguese attacked its capitals repeatedly and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-lama">Johor Lama</a> in 1587; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-sawar">Batu Sawar</a> in 1613 and carried members of the royal family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. Johor shifted its alliances accordingly, sending ships to help the Portuguese against Aceh's great attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1629. Its decisive move was an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, first concluded in 1606, which culminated in the joint siege that took Malacca from the Portuguese in January 1641. A long war with the Sumatran state of Jambi, which sacked Batu Sawar in 1673, was eventually won with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. In 1718 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a> of Siak seized the throne, and in 1722 Bugis warriors expelled him and gained great power at court. Under the Bugis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> Raja Haji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Malacca in 1784; Raja Haji was killed at Teluk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ketapang">Ketapang</a>, and the Dutch then occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a>. Thereafter Johor's forces were those of a small protected state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="1931 Constitution of Ethiopia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia">1931 Constitution of Ethiopia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1931_Constitution_of_Ethiopia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tekle Hawariat Tekle Mariyam Gedamu Woldegiorgis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultan, as heir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> line, stood at the apex of a hierarchy of great officials inherited from Malacca: the <em>bendahara</em> (chief minister), the <em>temenggong</em> (responsible for security and justice) and the <em>laksamana</em> (admiral), whose families often held real power. In the 1670s and 1680s the laksamana Tun Abdul Jamil dominated the state. The murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-ii-of-johor">Mahmud Shah II</a> in 1699, the last of the Malacca line, broke the sacred bond between ruler and subjects; the bendahara took the throne as Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-jalil-iv-of-johor">Abdul Jalil IV</a>, but many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a> questioned the new dynasty's legitimacy. From 1722 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> nobles held the hereditary office of <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-dipertuan-muda">Yang Dipertuan Muda</a></em> (under-king) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau</a>, controlling government and trade while the Malay sultans reigned. In the nineteenth century authority on the mainland passed to the temenggongs, whose line, after a treaty of 1855 with Sultan Ali, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor">Abu Bakar</a> (r. 1862–1895), recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as Sultan of Johor in 1885, created a modern administration with government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, a state council and the Johor constitution of 1895, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia">first written constitutions</a> in the Malay world. His son Sultan Ibrahim accepted a British General Adviser in 1914, bringing Johor into the Unfederated Malay States.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malay-annals.jpg" alt="Malay Annals" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispiece_of_a_Jawi_edition_of_the_Malay_Annals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> carried the political and cultural tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> into the modern era. Its court preserved Malacca's royal genealogy, regalia and literature: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Sejarah Melayu</a></em> (Malay Annals), the classic chronicle of the Malacca dynasty, is generally thought to have been revised for the Johor court in 1612, and the Bugis-Malay court of Riau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingga-islands">Lingga</a> became a centre of Malay letters in the nineteenth century, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ali-haji">Raja Ali Haji</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuhfat-al-nafis">Tuhfat al-Nafis</a></em> and grammars that helped standardise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-language">Malay language</a> from which modern Malay and Indonesian developed. Johor's alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1641 changed the balance of power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, and its dynastic disputes gave the British their opening to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1819. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a>, which divided Johor's realm along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore-strait">Singapore Strait</a>, fixed a line that still separates Malaysia and Singapore from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor">Abu Bakar</a>, Johor became a model of a modernising Malay monarchy, and in 1946 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-bahru">Johor Bahru</a> was the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> (UMNO), founded under the Johor chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onn-jaafar">Onn Jaafar</a> to oppose the Malayan Union. Several sultanates that broke away from Johor, among them Pahang, Terengganu and Riau-Lingga, trace their ruling houses to its court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles.jpg" alt="Stamford Raffles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Stamford Raffles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Francis_Joseph_-_Sir_Thomas_Stamford_Bingley_Raffles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Francis Joseph, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>'s transformation into a modern state began with the partition of its realm. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-iii-of-johor">Mahmud Shah III</a> died in 1812, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> faction installed his younger son Abdul Rahman, passing over the elder, Hussein. In 1819 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Stamford Raffles</a>, seeking a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a>, recognised Hussein as Sultan of Johor and signed a treaty with him and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenggong-abdul-rahman">Temenggong Abdul Rahman</a> to found a British settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, which was ceded outright in 1824. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> then divided the old kingdom: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingga-islands">Lingga</a>, under Sultan Abdul Rahman, fell into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sphere as the Riau-Lingga Sultanate, while the mainland lay in the British sphere. On the mainland the temenggongs, enriched by Singapore's trade, displaced Hussein's heirs; in 1855 Sultan Ali ceded the government of Johor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenggong-daeng-ibrahim">Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim</a>, whose son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor">Abu Bakar</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-bahru">Johor Bahru</a> his capital and took the title of sultan. Johor accepted a British adviser in 1914, joined the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> in 1948, which became independent in 1957, and became a state of Malaysia in 1963. It remains a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within the federation, and its ruler, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-iskandar-of-johor">Ibrahim Iskandar</a>, was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a>, King of Malaysia, taking office in January 2024.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Johor Sultanate, founded around 1528 by the heirs of the Malacca Sultanate, dominated the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> as a maritime trading state, helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1641, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> its foothold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and survives as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within Malaysia.</em></p>

